course: dictionary
lesson: 6178
title: Many meanings, one word: mint
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Mint is one word with several meanings, so you must check the words around it. These clues tell you which meaning is right. Try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: «Add a few leaves of mint to the salad.» Here mint is a plant with fresh, strong-tasting leaves. The clue is the word salad, because mint is used in cooking.
explain: «The Royal Mint will mint new coins for the summer.» The first mint is a place where a country makes its coins. The second mint is a verb: to mint means to make something, here coins.
explain: «The old stamp was in mint condition.» In mint condition means looking brand new. In UK slang, «That film is mint!» means it is excellent. Slang can change from place to place, so ask a grown-up or check a dictionary when you are unsure.
fact: Mint is a word with several meanings, so context clues help you find the right one.
fact: Mint is a plant with fresh, strong-tasting leaves, used in cooking and toothpaste; peppermint and spearmint are kinds of mint.
fact: A mint is a sweet that tastes of mint, and a mint is also a place where a country makes its coins, such as the Royal Mint.
fact: To mint coins means to make them, and «in mint condition» means looking brand new.
fact: In UK slang, «mint» means excellent, and in informal talk «a mint» can mean a lot of money.
exercise: «Add a few fresh leaves of mint to the salad.» What is mint here? | A kind of brand new condition | *A plant with fresh, strong-tasting leaves | A metal used for making coins | To make something new
exercise: «The Royal Mint makes all the coins in the UK.» What is the Royal Mint? | A sweet you suck slowly | *A place where a country makes its coins | A plant that grows in gardens | A bank that keeps money safe
exercise: Jayden says, «That game is mint!» What does mint mean? | Dirty and old | A cold, fresh taste | *Excellent, really great | To make coins
test: «The old coin was in mint condition, as if it had just left the factory.» What does mint condition mean? | Worth nothing | *Looking brand new | Covered in rust | Very small
test: «She popped a mint into her mouth and felt the cool tingle.» What is a mint here? | A leaf in a sauce | To make something | A coin from the bank | *A sweet that tastes of mint
exam: «The Royal Mint will mint new coins for the summer.» What does the second mint mean? | A sweet | Excellent | *To make coins | A plant with leaves
